    <div class="entry-content" set:html={`<div class="ast-post-format- single-layout-1"><div class="wp-block-columns is-layout-flex wp-container-core-columns-is-layout-7387b849 wp-block-columns-is-layout-flex"><div class="wp-block-column is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow"><figure class="wp-block-embed is-type-video is-provider-youtube wp-block-embed-youtube wp-embed-aspect-16-9 wp-has-aspect-ratio"><div class="wp-block-embed__wrapper">
<iframe data-lazyloaded="1" src="about:blank" title="Daily Lab Routine: Feather Sample DNA Testing | Bird Sex Identification Process" width="1200" height="675" data-litespeed-src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/Wkg0FkoDwfA?feature=oembed" frameborder="0" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share" referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" allowfullscreen></iframe></div></figure></div></div><div class="wp-block-columns is-layout-flex wp-container-core-columns-is-layout-7387b849 wp-block-columns-is-layout-flex"><div class="wp-block-column is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow"><p>This video demonstrates the complete<a href="/avian-gender-dna-test/"> avian DNA sexing</a> workflow used in modern laboratories. The process begins with proper sample collection. Fresh feathers containing intact follicles are carefully prepared because the follicle contains living cells needed for DNA extraction. In some cases, blood samples may also be used for higher DNA concentration.</p><p>After collection, the laboratory performs DNA extraction to isolate genetic material from the sample. Accurate extraction is critical because poor-quality DNA can affect PCR amplification and lead to unclear results. Once purified DNA is obtained, the sample is transferred into a PCR reaction system containing primers, enzymes, and amplification reagents.</p><p>During PCR amplification, target regions of bird DNA are copied repeatedly inside the thermal cycler. These genetic markers allow laboratories to identify sex chromosomes associated with male or female birds. Following amplification, gel electrophoresis is used to visualize DNA bands. By analyzing the band patterns, technicians can determine whether the bird is male or female.</p><p>The video also highlights the importance of contamination control, proper laboratory handling, and accurate interpretation of electrophoresis results. Even small procedural errors can influence final results, which is why professional laboratory protocols remain essential for reliable avian DNA testing.</p><p>PCR-based bird sexing is now widely used by breeders, pet owners, conservation programs, and racing pigeon enthusiasts because it offers a scientific and non-invasive alternative to traditional gender identification methods.</p></div></div><p></p></div></div>`} />
